text:
Plug-in estimators
Given a sample y_1,\dots,y_n with mean \bar y:

\widehat{T}_1 = \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\frac{y_i}{\bar y}\log\frac{y_i}{\bar y}
= \frac{\sum_i y_i\log y_i}{\sum_i y_i} - \log\bar y,
\qquad
\widehat{T}_0 = \log\bar y - \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\log y_i .

T1.hat &lt;- function(y) {
  m &lt;- mean(y)
  mean((y / m) * log(y / m))
}

T0.hat &lt;- function(y) {
  log(mean(y)) - mean(log(y))
}
These estimators are consistent but biased in finite samples. The bias is negligible for symmetric, low-inequality data, but becomes severe under strong skewness and inequality. See the interactive bias explorer.

This page runs a small Monte Carlo experiment in your browser. For each true value of the index, it draws R samples of size n from the selected distributions, computes the plug-in estimator and reports the empirical relative bias:

RB(\%) = 100 \times \frac{\frac{1}{R}\sum_{r=1}^{R}\widehat{T}^{(r)} - T}{T}.

The parameters of each distribution are set so that the true index (T_0 or T_1) takes the value on the horizontal axis. Pareto is the most skewed distribution, Gamma the least.


