About THEILUMINA
THEILUMINA is a research project proposal led by the University of Granada (Spain) that develops new methods to estimate Theil inequality indices (T_0 and T_1) and to make reliable inferences about them. It builds on the previous project GiniUs (PID2022-136235NB-I00) on the Gini index, which produced the R package giniVarCI.
Theil indices are widely used because, unlike the Gini index, they can be additively decomposed into inequality between groups and inequality within groups. However, their estimators can be severely biased, and their confidence intervals unreliable, when data are highly skewed and unequal, which is precisely where measuring inequality matters most. The research problems and questions page illustrates these problems with preliminary results.
The project:

diagnoses, predicts and corrects the bias of the estimators of T_0 and T_1;
provides reliable variance estimators and confidence intervals, based on the jackknife;
reduces the computational cost of the jackknife to make inference feasible with large samples;
develops the open-source R package theil, with a C++ core;
transfers these methods to water economics, tourism, servitization and digital transformation of firms, and global convergence.
